A command written for the second drive of a channel that has only the first one was ignored and the Status
register read 00h for ever after: the first of the two ways ATA-3 gives a lone Device 0 ("Single device
configurations, Device 0 only"). A driver that probes both drives with IDENTIFY DEVICE and tells a missing one
by the error bit then waits its whole timeout for DRQ: a 32-bit protected-mode disk driver takes a second for
each channel at every start, two seconds with a disk and a CD drive, to learn what the error bit says at once.
Now the second way: the channel keeps a Status and an Error register for the missing drive; a command for it
other than EXECUTE DEVICE DIAGNOSTIC and INITIALIZE DEVICE PARAMETERS sets ERR in the one and ABRT in the
other (Status reads 01h), a reset or a write of the drive select register clears them. No interrupt is raised
(the standard's second way raises one when nIEN is clear; none came before either, and a driver that waits
for one does as it did). A channel with no drive at all, or with the second drive only, is unchanged, and so
are the registers a detection reads (sector count, LBA: 00h), so nothing new is found.

